    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Concept Overview</h2>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 1.5rem;">
            <li><strong>Serialization</strong>: Converting a live object into a JSON-compatible dictionary (`to_save_data`).</li>
            <li><strong>Deserialization</strong>: Creating a new object from a dictionary (`from_save_data`).</li>
            <li><strong>Factory Method</strong>: `from_save_data` is a `@classmethod` because we don't have an object yet! We are <em>making</em> one.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <div class="info-content">
            <p><strong>1. `cls` vs `self`</strong></p>
            <p>In `from_save_data`, `self` doesn't exist. We use `cls(...)` to call the constructor. This is often the hardest concept for students.</p>
            
            <p><strong>2. Restoring Inventory</strong></p>
            <p>The saved data is a list of strings (`["key", "map"]`). The new player needs a <em>new</em> `Inventory` object, and we must loop through the list to `add()` them back.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Solution Code</h2>
        <div class="code-block" style="background: #1e1e1e; color: #d4d4d4; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto;">
            <pre><code>    def to_save_data(self):
        return {
            "name": self.name,
            "player_type": "player", # or "brute"/"scout" (super call handles this)
            "health": self.health,
            "max_health": self.max_health,
            "armour": self.armour,
            "accuracy": self.accuracy,
            "inventory": self.inventory.list_items() # Extract string list
        }

    @classmethod
    def from_save_data(cls, data):
        # 1. Create instance using name
        player = cls(data.get("name", "Unknown"))
        
        # 2. Restore Stats
        player.health = data.get("health", 100)
        player.max_health = data.get("max_health", 100)
        player.armour = data.get("armour", 2)
        player.accuracy = data.get("accuracy", 85)
        
        # 3. Restore Inventory
        # Ensure imports are handled or Inventory is available
        from .inventory import Inventory
        player.inventory = Inventory()
        for item_id in data.get("inventory", []):
            player.inventory.add(item_id)
            
        return player</code></pre>
        </div>
    </section>

